WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.250
Welcome to the last chapter of
Building Ticket-to-Machine.

2
00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:05.862
Chapter 8: one writer per attribute.

3
00:00:05.862 --> 00:00:09.437
Two writers on one attribute
isn't drift you can fix.

4
00:00:09.437 --> 00:00:10.900
It's drift you've scheduled.

5
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:15.870
Book 3 makes Entra inbound provisioning
the one writer into the directory,

6
00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:18.675
but SailPoint can also provision Entra ID.

7
00:00:18.675 --> 00:00:20.253
If both write department,

8
00:00:20.253 --> 00:00:24.237
the reconciliation reports drift
that the next sync recreates.

9
00:00:24.237 --> 00:00:28.144
This chapter compares the two
provisioning configurations,

10
00:00:28.144 --> 00:00:31.438
offline, and names every
attribute with two writers.

11
00:00:31.438 --> 00:00:34.140
Then two labs and the dangers.

12
00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.040
Why does this matter?

13
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:44.036
The example guide's fourth gap: SailPoint
reads the HR system and can provision

14
00:00:44.036 --> 00:00:48.878
the directory, while Book 3 makes Entra
inbound provisioning the one writer.

15
00:00:48.878 --> 00:00:52.114
The guide's advice is to pick
one writer per attribute.

16
00:00:52.114 --> 00:00:57.267
Either Entra inbound provisioning stays the
door and SailPoint governs entitlements,

17
00:00:57.267 --> 00:01:00.090
or SailPoint becomes the
provisioning service.

18
00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:01.065
Not both.

19
00:01:01.065 --> 00:01:04.349
Until now, the drift client
saw only the symptom,

20
00:01:04.349 --> 00:01:07.465
drift that keeps coming
back, never the cause.

21
00:01:07.465 --> 00:01:13.250
Both configurations export as JSON,
so the check can run on files, today.

22
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.188
Why only this one?

23
00:01:18.188 --> 00:01:22.876
The kit plans five items for Pass 2,
and four of them need more than files.

24
00:01:22.876 --> 00:01:25.356
One approver per authority class needs the

25
00:01:25.356 --> 00:01:28.112
catalog app's internals,
which aren't documented.

26
00:01:28.112 --> 00:01:32.575
Change records on demand need a
ServiceNow flow on a live instance.

27
00:01:32.575 --> 00:01:35.121
The SailPoint half of the evidence export

28
00:01:35.121 --> 00:01:37.812
needs event retention
nobody has confirmed.

29
00:01:37.812 --> 00:01:41.425
And email intake needs a
mailbox and an inbound action.

30
00:01:41.425 --> 00:01:45.870
The writer check needs only two
documented exports compared offline,

31
00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.230
so it can be built and tested now.

32
00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:56.580
The first export is the inbound
provisioning job's schema, read from Graph.

33
00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:00.118
The check takes the rules that
target the Entra directory,

34
00:02:00.118 --> 00:02:04.392
by default named Azure Active Directory,
the name Learn's examples use.

35
00:02:04.392 --> 00:02:07.873
Inside them it takes the
enabled object mappings to User,

36
00:02:07.873 --> 00:02:11.205
and each attribute mapping's
target name and flow type.

37
00:02:11.205 --> 00:02:16.759
Two flow types matter: Always, the default,
updates the attribute on every change,

38
00:02:16.759 --> 00:02:20.980
and object-add-only sets it
only when the user is created.

39
00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:28.721
The second export is the SailPoint
source's provisioning policies,

40
00:02:28.721 --> 00:02:30.930
for the source that writes to Entra ID.

41
00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:34.517
Each policy has a usage type
and a list of field names.

42
00:02:34.517 --> 00:02:39.218
The update profile is used for attribute
sync, so it counts as an update.

43
00:02:39.218 --> 00:02:42.130
The create profile is used
when the account is made.

44
00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:44.954
Enable, disable and the
other types don't write

45
00:02:44.954 --> 00:02:47.630
user attributes this
way, so they're ignored.

46
00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:52.124
One catch to remember: SailPoint's
field names are the connector's,

47
00:02:52.124 --> 00:02:54.310
not necessarily Entra ID's.

48
00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:02.273
For each attribute both systems
write, the check assigns a severity.

49
00:03:02.273 --> 00:03:03.990
Both update it: a Finding,

50
00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:08.198
because each overwrites the
other and drift never settles.

51
00:03:08.198 --> 00:03:11.323
One updates it and the
other sets it at creation:

52
00:03:11.323 --> 00:03:14.048
a Warning, two sources
for its first value.

53
00:03:14.048 --> 00:03:16.748
Both set it only at creation: Info.

54
00:03:16.748 --> 00:03:18.748
Any Finding fails the command.

55
00:03:18.748 --> 00:03:21.834
Names are matched ignoring
case, and an alias maps a

56
00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:25.360
SailPoint field to its Entra
attribute where they differ.

57
00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:30.570
The check never guesses that mapping;
the alias list is a reviewed input.

58
00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:39.739
Six tests cover flow types, usage types
and aliases, the three severities, disabled

59
00:03:39.739 --> 00:03:45.158
mappings being ignored, a wrong directory
name being refused, and the command line.

60
00:03:45.158 --> 00:03:48.484
The usual caution applies,
and it matters more here:

61
00:03:48.484 --> 00:03:50.782
the check reads fictional exports.

62
00:03:50.782 --> 00:03:54.696
It can't show that your schema names
the Entra directory the same way,

63
00:03:54.696 --> 00:03:57.282
or that SailPoint's field
names match Entra's.

64
00:03:57.282 --> 00:04:03.510
Run Lab 2 on your own exports and review
the alias list before acting on any result.

65
00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:10.747
The facts behind the exports,
read on November first.

66
00:04:10.747 --> 00:04:15.288
Reading a synchronization schema is
available in the government clouds; oddly,

67
00:04:15.288 --> 00:04:19.690
Learn lists a custom security attribute
permission as the least-privileged

68
00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:24.573
application permission, with Synchronization
dot Read dot All among the higher ones.

69
00:04:24.573 --> 00:04:29.123
The schema's shape is rules, object
mappings and attribute mappings.

70
00:04:29.123 --> 00:04:34.449
SailPoint's policies come from the
provisioning-policies endpoint with a read or

71
00:04:34.449 --> 00:04:39.461
manage scope, at the org admin user level,
each with a name, usage type and fields.

72
00:04:39.461 --> 00:04:42.930
GCC uses the worldwide Graph host.

73
00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:50.130
Four points are open, and each one
changes what the check can see.

74
00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:54.036
Why Learn names a custom security
attribute permission as the least

75
00:04:54.036 --> 00:04:57.530
privilege for reading a schema;
confirm the read works with it.

76
00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:01.291
Which directory name an HR
inbound job actually uses;

77
00:05:01.291 --> 00:05:05.480
Learn's pages differ, so the check
refuses a name it can't find.

78
00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:09.210
How SailPoint's connector
fields map to Entra attributes;

79
00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:11.230
that's why aliases are reviewed.

80
00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:15.588
And whether SailPoint writes Entra
attributes through other paths,

81
00:05:15.588 --> 00:05:17.193
transforms or workflows.

82
00:05:17.193 --> 00:05:19.470
The check covers policies only.

83
00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:24.383
What this proves.

84
00:05:24.383 --> 00:05:29.320
Each Entra user attribute has one
authoritative writer, or the overlap is named

85
00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:34.557
and assigned to someone, which supports
configuration settings and change control.

86
00:05:34.557 --> 00:05:38.177
Drift that the next sync would
recreate is traced to its cause,

87
00:05:38.177 --> 00:05:40.520
which makes the monitoring meaningful.

88
00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:45.813
And a change to either provisioning
configuration is checked before it lands,

89
00:05:45.813 --> 00:05:49.070
which is change control
and impact analysis, CM-4.

90
00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:51.420
The evidence is the check's output and the

91
00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:54.260
data architect's decision
for each Finding.

92
00:05:58.010 --> 00:05:59.060
Who's involved?

93
00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:02.616
Identity engineers who own
the inbound provisioning job

94
00:06:02.616 --> 00:06:06.172
or the SailPoint Entra source,
and produce the two exports.

95
00:06:06.172 --> 00:06:11.326
The data architect, who decides which
system owns each attribute and reviews

96
00:06:11.326 --> 00:06:16.398
the aliases; Book 3's default keeps
Entra inbound provisioning as the writer.

97
00:06:16.398 --> 00:06:19.673
And the I S S O, who keeps the decisions.

98
00:06:19.673 --> 00:06:21.447
The two reads are read-only.

99
00:06:21.447 --> 00:06:27.097
One wrinkle: SailPoint's policies endpoint
documents the org admin user level,

100
00:06:27.097 --> 00:06:32.680
so a personal token is the wrong tool;
use an API client with one read scope.

101
00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:37.242
Lab 1.

102
00:06:37.242 --> 00:06:38.630
Six tests pass.

103
00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:41.592
Then run the check on the
two fictional exports.

104
00:06:41.592 --> 00:06:46.468
Entra inbound provisioning writes eight
attributes and SailPoint writes seven.

105
00:06:46.468 --> 00:06:49.657
Department is a Finding:
both systems update it,

106
00:06:49.657 --> 00:06:53.255
so each overwrites the other
and drift never settles.

107
00:06:53.255 --> 00:06:57.943
Display name is a Warning: one updates
it and the other sets it at creation.

108
00:06:57.943 --> 00:07:01.717
Two attributes are only Info,
set by both at creation.

109
00:07:01.717 --> 00:07:04.380
The command fails because of that Finding.

110
00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:12.393
Now map SailPoint's title field to
Entra's job title and run it again.

111
00:07:12.393 --> 00:07:16.180
A second Finding appears: job
title has two writers too.

112
00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:18.825
Without the alias that
writer was invisible,

113
00:07:18.825 --> 00:07:21.255
which is why the alias list gets reviewed.

114
00:07:21.255 --> 00:07:23.655
Next, give the wrong directory name.

115
00:07:23.655 --> 00:07:26.464
The check refuses and exits with an error,

116
00:07:26.464 --> 00:07:29.355
rather than passing quietly as no overlap.

117
00:07:29.355 --> 00:07:31.899
Finally, list the reads the exports need:

118
00:07:31.899 --> 00:07:37.490
one SailPoint read scope for the policies,
and one Graph permission for the schema.

119
00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:46.133
Lab 2 uses your own exports, from a test
tenant with an inbound provisioning

120
00:07:46.133 --> 00:07:50.790
job and a SailPoint sandbox, never
production; the authors haven't run it.

121
00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:52.352
Both reads are GET only.

122
00:07:52.352 --> 00:07:56.639
Export the job's schema and note
the directory name it actually uses.

123
00:07:56.639 --> 00:08:02.528
Export the SailPoint source's policies
with an API client holding one read scope.

124
00:08:02.528 --> 00:08:05.156
Build the alias list from
the source's account

125
00:08:05.156 --> 00:08:07.715
schema and have the data
architect review it.

126
00:08:07.715 --> 00:08:11.378
Run the check, and hand each
Finding to the data architect.

127
00:08:11.378 --> 00:08:12.710
Then clean up.

128
00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:17.485
The dangers.

129
00:08:17.485 --> 00:08:21.077
Two systems updating one
attribute means drift fixed

130
00:08:21.077 --> 00:08:24.510
and recreated forever; that's
a Finding and a failure.

131
00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:28.935
Field names that differ can hide a
second writer, so aliases are reviewed.

132
00:08:28.935 --> 00:08:34.010
A wrong directory name would make every
attribute look unwritten, so it's refused.

133
00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:37.773
A disabled mapping would raise
false findings, so it's skipped.

134
00:08:37.773 --> 00:08:42.948
An export with values in it may carry
personal data, so export mappings only.

135
00:08:42.948 --> 00:08:46.438
And writers outside provisioning
policies aren't seen at all,

136
00:08:46.438 --> 00:08:48.770
so a person confirms there are none.

137
00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:53.720
Done looks like this.

138
00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:57.620
The check runs on current exports
with the reviewed alias list.

139
00:08:57.620 --> 00:09:01.503
Every Finding has a decision,
which system owns the attribute,

140
00:09:01.503 --> 00:09:03.482
with the other mapping removed.

141
00:09:03.482 --> 00:09:07.920
Every Warning is understood, so
each first value has one source.

142
00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:10.558
The directory name
matches the job's schema.

143
00:09:10.558 --> 00:09:13.769
The two export identities
hold only their reads.

144
00:09:13.769 --> 00:09:15.183
And the tests pass.

145
00:09:15.183 --> 00:09:20.043
That completes the workbook: the typed
task, two least-privilege clients,

146
00:09:20.043 --> 00:09:23.770
three builds, the evidence,
and one writer per attribute.

147
00:09:23.770 --> 00:09:24.920
Thanks for watching.
